"Major simplification (2026-08-12, user): COUNTED PROPERTIES ARE JUST STATS. StatBlock is already a secador-stamped list of signed i32 stats [attack, mhp, defense, mep] with AddAssign merge, asset authoring (StatBlockAsset), baseline+trait summation, dirty-flag recompute, and client subscription. Each countable property (arm, hand, gait, reach, bladed, pole, blunt, ...) becomes one more entry in that seca_int list. Mapping: PROVIDERS = positive contributions (baselines/traits carry base slots -- where they belong; stances, armaments, relations, status effects contribute their own stat blocks into the existing total pipeline). CONSUMERS = negative contributions (chain-on-arm: hand -1; grips: each one-handed blade hand -1). VALIDATION = nonnegativity of the summed block over the checked properties (2 hands cannot equip 3 one-handed blades because hand would go negative). REQUIREMENTS = component-wise >= threshold check, one new stamped op (meets()). PREVIEWS = hypothetical stat blocks: base + traits + candidate armaments + candidate stance summed from the same subscribed assets, identical math client and server. Signed ints natively express strange statuses (temporary extra arms = arm +1 with status lifecycle). Efficiency deferred until performance testing (user). NO separate property asset kind at all -- properties are explicit typed fields (fits no-magic-values). Note: StatBlock's id-vec stats already carry action_ids -- precedent for modifier-granted content, though the derived action set still filters via requirements. WRINKLE for the data model: requirements must state WHICH stats they check explicitly (a requirement struct of named thresholds), not infer 'unchecked' from zero -- stats are signed, so a debuffed attack of -1 must not fail a technique that never meant to check attack. SIZE (user): a granular signed size stat instead of inherent categories like gargantuan. Forcing contests become DIFFERENCES (climb override resisted when target.size - climber.size >= threshold); kaiju and fairy battles are the same mechanics from different perspectives; navigation gains size-gated passages; changing the player's size is itself new experience space. WHAT DOES NOT COLLAPSE into stats: modifier lifecycle (stance exclusivity + forced-transition contests, relation target refs + authored exits, status durations), per-account technique slot assignment, and the derived <=6 action set computation (active stance -> assigned techniques -> requirement check vs total stat block), which hooks the existing dirties() machinery since stat-contributing components already declare flags."